WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.069 --> 00:00:03.569
Hi, my name is Chris Gagne and I'm an Agile coach.

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:07.120
Today, I will talk about I.N.V.E.S.T.

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:14.180
I.N.V.E.S.T. is a mnemonic that helps us remember a good starting point for the Definition of Ready.

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:19.060
The Definition of Ready is a documented team agreement.

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:27.000
It defines the conditions that must be met for a product backlog item to be considered ready to work on by the team.

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:30.240
It's not a formal contract or approval gate

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:36.000
but rather a set of guidelines that will help your team make a good trade-off between

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:39.360
minimizing risks and getting started.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:47.320
Creating and following the Definition of Ready diligently can easily double the speed of an Agile team.

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:54.280
That's because the team can stay focused on shipping working software not chasing down requirements.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:01:02.760
For a real-world example, think of the "mise en place" or "everything in its place" concept used by chefs and cooking shows.

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:09.680
Before they begin preparing the recipe, they carefully organize and measure the various ingredients

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:16.170
so they're immediately at hand while cooking. 
A little careful preparation goes a long way

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:18.880
So what's I.N.V.E.S.T?

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:24.000
Well, I like to add a "U" in front for U.-I.N.V.E.S.T but you don't have to.

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:29.820
"U" stands for "User Experience" which includes everything from research,

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:34.140
wireframes, mocks, written content, and even visual design.

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:38.340
Now mature Scrum teams with dedicated designers 
can research,

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:45.160
design, code, test, and ship within 
a single two week sprint.

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:51.240
If you can't, consider making "User Experience" a prerequisite for development work beginning.

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:54.520
"I" means "Independent."

00:01:54.520 --> 00:02:02.360
The backlog item should be self-contained in a way that there is no in sprint dependency 
on another backlog item.

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:06.180
"N" means "Negotiable."

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:12.120
Until the Sprint formally starts, the development team and product owner can negotiate

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:14.540
on the nature and scope of the item.

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:20.740
Once the Sprint starts, the product owner should not materially change the acceptance criteria.

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:24.540
"V" means "Valuable."

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:28.280
A backlog item must deliver value to the stakeholders.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:33.540
If you can't clearly articulate the value for the work, 
you shouldn't do it.

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:36.480
"E" means "Estimable."

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:43.160
The team must be able to estimate the size of the backlog item ideally using story points.

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:47.620
Otherwise, you won't be able to effectively manage 
your flow of work

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:50.960
"S" means "Small."

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:56.360
Ideally, the backlog item should not take the team more than half a sprint to complete.

00:02:56.620 --> 00:02:59.240
"T" means "Testable."

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:04.900
The backlog item should include enough information to make test development possible.

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:11.260
For instance, "the coffee should be between 120 and 130 degrees Fahrenheit"

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:16.960
is a clear acceptance criteria than 
"the coffee should be hot."

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:22.280
Well now you know what I.N.V.E.S.T is! 
Go create a Definition of Ready with your team.

